🚂 …choo choo.

Or, given what we’ve been talking about:

tokens → embeddings
chuga → attention
chuga → attention
choo → output token

Every “chuga” is another pass of computation—gathering context, propagating information, refining the hidden state. The “choo” is the visible token that finally comes out.

It’s actually a decent metaphor:

Input
  │
[chuga]  Layer 1
  │
[chuga]  Layer 2
  │
[chuga]  Layer 3
  │
...
  │
🚂 choo  (next token emitted)

The train isn’t carrying the answer fully formed. It’s laying track as it moves, while being guided by the terrain (context) and the engine (the model’s learned weights).

So your “chuga chuga” made me smile because, in a simplified way, that’s what inference feels like: thousands of tiny numerical “chugas” before every visible “choo.”